---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
import { COUNTRIES, DATA_SOURCES, DATA_GENERATED_AT, CAPITALS_POOL, COMPARE_POOL } from '../lib/countries';
import { OUTLINE_POOL } from '../lib/outlines';

const title = `About ${site.name}`;
const description = `How ${site.name}'s three daily geography games work, where the country data comes from, and how the daily puzzle is chosen.`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="container container--narrow section prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'About', url: '/about/' }]} />
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="small muted">Data last updated: {DATA_GENERATED_AT}</p>
    <p>{site.description}</p>

    <h2>The three games</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Outline</strong> — a daily silhouette drawn from a curated pool of {OUTLINE_POOL.length} countries large and distinct enough to be recognisable by shape alone; 6 guesses, with distance, compass direction and a proximity percentage after each one.</li>
      <li><strong>Capitals</strong> — 10 multiple-choice rounds a day drawn from {CAPITALS_POOL.length} countries with a recorded capital, mixing "capital → country" and "country → capital" questions.</li>
      <li><strong>Bigger or smaller</strong> — 10 rounds a day comparing population or land area between two of {COMPARE_POOL.length} countries with both figures on record.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the daily puzzle is chosen</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing is random and nothing is server-generated: the puzzle for each mode is derived entirely in your browser from the
      current UTC date. Each mode's country pool is shuffled once with a seeded algorithm (so the order looks random but is
      fixed), and the day's puzzle number selects the next country in that fixed order — so the full pool has to be exhausted
      before any country repeats, and everyone who opens the page on the same UTC day sees the same puzzle. Puzzle #1 is{' '}
      {site.epoch} (days before that are puzzle #0 and lower). Practice links from the <a href={withBase("/archive/")}>archive</a> or a "challenge a friend" button reuse this same
      mechanism with a past date or a random seed, and never affect your streak.
    </p>

    <h2>Distance, direction and proximity (Outline mode)</h2>
    <p>
      Distance is the <strong>great-circle distance</strong> between the geographic centre points (roughly the label point Natural
      Earth uses for each country) of your guess and the answer, using the haversine formula on a spherical Earth
      (radius 6,371.0088&nbsp;km). Direction is that same bearing, snapped to the nearest of 8 compass points. Proximity is a
      percentage that is 100% for an exact match and falls smoothly to 0% at the antipodal point (halfway around the world),
      so a guess on the correct continent still scores meaningfully higher than one on the far side of the planet.
    </p>

    <h2>Data sources & licences</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Source</th><th>Used for</th><th>Licence</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Natural Earth 1:50m Admin 0 — Countries</td><td>Country outline shapes, adjacency (shared borders), continent/subregion grouping, and the population/area fallback below</td><td>Public domain</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wikidata</td><td>Capitals, population, area, ISO codes, coordinates</td><td>CC0 (public domain dedication)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">{DATA_SOURCES.naturalEarth} · {DATA_SOURCES.wikidata}.</p>
    <p>
      Outline shapes are simplified (Douglas–Peucker simplification) from the Natural Earth polygons and re-projected with an
      azimuthal equal-area projection centred on each country, so shapes are proportionate and not stretched by their
      position on the globe. A small number of far-flung overseas islands are dropped from a country's outline where keeping
      them would make the silhouette harder to draw at this resolution rather than more recognisable — the fact pages'
      coordinates and area figures still cover the whole country.
    </p>
    <p>
      Population figures use each place's most recent Wikidata population statement dated 2022 or later. Where no such
      recent statement exists, the figure instead falls back to Natural Earth's own population estimate — a single,
      older snapshot (year shown next to the figure on each country's page, since it isn't the same year for every
      place). Either way, treat every population and area figure as a single point-in-time snapshot, not live data.
      Some entries (dependent territories such as Hong Kong or Macao) do not have their own head-of-state capital in
      the strict sense and are marked accordingly rather than guessed at. Countries with more than one capital city
      (for example South Africa or Bolivia) show the primary capital used for the games, with the constitutional
      detail noted on that country's page.
    </p>

    <h2>Fairness of the daily seed</h2>
    <p>
      Because the seed is a deterministic function of the public UTC date and mode name — not of who is asking or from
      where — every player sees the same puzzle on the same day, and nobody (including us) can see tomorrow's answer early or
      target a specific player with an easier or harder puzzle.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy by design</h2>
    <p>
      {site.name} runs entirely in your browser. Guesses, scores and streaks are not uploaded anywhere; they live in your
      browser's local storage until you clear it or export a backup. See the <a href={withBase("/privacy/")}>privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Who maintains it</h2>
    <p>
      Built and maintained by <a href={site.author.url} rel="noopener">{site.author.name}</a>. Launched {site.launched}. Found a
      data error or a bug? <a href={withBase("/contact/")}>Get in touch</a> with the exact page and the correct figure plus a source, and
      we'll fix it.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
